Z||000017||Oreif||08-29-2000||10:44 AM||||To see if timing chain "jumped" On the 4cyl you would need to access the timing chain to be 100% sure. OR Basically you can set the crank shaft to 0 on the timing marks (by hand) then look at the #1 cylinders rocker arms. They should be closed. If not turn crank around to 0 again.<BR>(for every 1 turn of the cam is 2 turns of the crank) If at the next 0 the rockers are not fully closed then you have a timing chain problem. When the crank hits 0 you are either at full compression or at the end of exhaust/start of intake stroke. You want to check it on the compression stroke. <BR>If timing chain jumped it will be more than a tooth. I don't remember but I thought the timing gears were steel Jumping is hard to do. Try this, put a socket on the crank bolt and a large ratchet and move the engine back and forth slightly. The cam should move the same time as the crank. If it looks like there is some slop you may have "stretched" the chain.<BR>Hope this helps.||||reg||1
